=> How to deal with spacing/alignment issue in awk command?

There is no extra spacing in your output because awk is replacing the original whitespace with one space.

Your command:

df -h | awk '{print $1,$5}'

Here:

$1 → Filesystem
$5 → Use%
, → tells awk to put the output field separator (OFS) between them.
Default OFS is one space.

So:

Filesystem Use%
none 0%
none 1%
If you want more spacing

You can set OFS yourself:

df -h | awk 'BEGIN {OFS="    "} {print $1,$5}'

But here the problem is Why the spacing looks different?

You set:

OFS="        "

That means 8 spaces between $1 and $5.

So awk produces:

Filesystem        Use%
none        0%
drivers        78%
/dev/sdd        1%

The solution is for a properly aligned table, use printf:

df -h | awk '{printf "%-20s %s\n", $1, $5}'

Output will look more like:

Filesystem           Use%
none                 0%
none                 1%
drivers              78%
/dev/sdd             1%
rootfs               1%
C:\                  78%
tmpfs                0%

=> Understand %-20s
%    → format
-    → left align
20   → give 20 characters of space
s    → string

